      {
        "id": "lem-adagrad-accumulated-gram-matrix-is-psd",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "AdaGrad accumulated Gram matrices and both scales are positive semidefinite",
        "deps": [
          "alg-adagrad",
          "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
          "def-transpose-of-a-matrix",
          "lem-of-sign-rules",
          "lem-psd-matrix-has-unique-psd-square-root",
          "thm-of-square-roots"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "statement": "For the AdaGrad inputs, at each round $t\\in\\{0,\\ldots,T\\}$ the accumulated matrix $G_t=\\sum_{s=1}^t g_sg_s^\\top$, with $G_0=0$, is a finite real symmetric positive-semidefinite matrix. More precisely, for every $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$,\n$$v^\\top G_tv=\\sum_{s=1}^t(g_s^\\top v)^2\\ge0, \\qquad (G_t)_{ii}=\\sum_{s=1}^t g_{s,i}^2\\ge0.$$\nHere the coordinate and matrix-entry labels $i,j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ are one-based: $g_{s,i}=g_s(i-1)$ and $(G_t)_{ij}=G_t(i-1,j-1)$ in the underlying zero-based conventions. Empty sums have value zero. Therefore both the full-matrix scale $G_t^{1/2}$ and the diagonal scale $\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{(G_t)_{11}},\\ldots,\\sqrt{(G_t)_{dd}})$ exist and are finite real symmetric positive-semidefinite matrices. Singular and zero accumulated matrices are allowed."
      },
